[{:db/id "q-109279/selection/7b83b1e95c83c5331f3bcd8ddf7343b45a7a2f21b360f802c825a067915477b6" :answer/id #uuid "eb5b4cd9-b88c-59aa-9d54-b8c464a4e2ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=e\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109279/selection/59669f0e160d1b7287db5f906150a03c8439dd8a048442ac0161f40ab8883616" :answer/id #uuid "a9fe76ac-5201-5635-a26d-139134840c5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=1\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109279/selection/e3529c5097809598f64063bcc942331aecc2d81cd8b03b4f4745066798f1cc8a" :answer/id #uuid "e23b69c1-b7ec-544c-9288-68b145b0b595" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109279/selection/d5def0cf746ec332768f9da3bbd3918000db01e741698aca0cf711527ac7cb72" :answer/id #uuid "6d472fbb-2846-5a9b-b80c-7f19d79a6a4b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=2\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109279/selection/41829d46bcc1a729617a6ae04c4cbfbd0a7ef13db1e25d97ab9958eed72c0a2a" :answer/id #uuid "de14590c-5cb1-55d7-a957-286f17bd17e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=\\frac{1}{e}\\,\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109279/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bae1f563-1213-5317-ba01-40ee04d62d05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-109279/selection/59669f0e160d1b7287db5f906150a03c8439dd8a048442ac0161f40ab8883616" :answer-field/choices ["q-109279/selection/7b83b1e95c83c5331f3bcd8ddf7343b45a7a2f21b360f802c825a067915477b6" "q-109279/selection/59669f0e160d1b7287db5f906150a03c8439dd8a048442ac0161f40ab8883616" "q-109279/selection/e3529c5097809598f64063bcc942331aecc2d81cd8b03b4f4745066798f1cc8a" "q-109279/selection/d5def0cf746ec332768f9da3bbd3918000db01e741698aca0cf711527ac7cb72" "q-109279/selection/41829d46bcc1a729617a6ae04c4cbfbd0a7ef13db1e25d97ab9958eed72c0a2a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109279" :question/id #uuid "a2aa3e69-2969-5499-b4e6-a1c16d2ad5a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109279" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)={ln}^{2}⁡t+2,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary." :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({ln}^{2}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & =0 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109279/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109279"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335684/field-1/a272cbe64fea514fe5c8c9685aef2c9378fdfe44c54941c8bcba64a5f713bc84" :answer/id #uuid "b701c2c8-bb36-5117-92ac-ed5e9ae1e4c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335684/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "588f6fb1-a586-5def-97d8-bf3fabd6137d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335684/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a3861490-cd42-533c-a625-98fe523eb3ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335684/field-1/a272cbe64fea514fe5c8c9685aef2c9378fdfe44c54941c8bcba64a5f713bc84" :answer-field/choices ["q-335684/field-1/a272cbe64fea514fe5c8c9685aef2c9378fdfe44c54941c8bcba64a5f713bc84" "q-335684/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335684" :question/id #uuid "d8ef2406-47cb-5949-bfc7-3df8354686c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335684" :question/problem "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8t-7}{2\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8t-7}{2\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}} & =0 \\\\ 8t-7 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335684/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335684"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335687/field-1/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer/id #uuid "d58b7edb-43ad-5310-96a1-f4d5e82fc075" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335687/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d12f94d-a237-5802-87b3-e74c6daf96ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335687/field-1/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856" :answer-field/choices ["q-335687/field-1/4280bcd0914e40dbdc039bc97ee5d5baafd2654e75e9b692316d9c28c4842856"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335687" :question/id #uuid "476287a9-db73-54d5-ae8c-35bc0889d0f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-335687" :question/problem "The position $x(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)=2{t}^{2}-10t+3,\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-10t+3) \\\\  & =4t-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-10 & =0 \\\\ 4t & =10 \\\\ t & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{5}{2}\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335687/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335687"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335696/field-1/be7671dfcec88fd83021721c027e180f94aa5dc830cb53553598c14eaf369bc4" :answer/id #uuid "132f0e53-8a2c-5262-8eeb-c3e9fe48f0ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335696/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6cce752b-5ee1-5083-a80a-796649da076b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335696/field-1/be7671dfcec88fd83021721c027e180f94aa5dc830cb53553598c14eaf369bc4" :answer-field/choices ["q-335696/field-1/be7671dfcec88fd83021721c027e180f94aa5dc830cb53553598c14eaf369bc4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335696" :question/id #uuid "a20eefee-8104-50ec-9236-d64413e98bff" :question/math-academy-id "q-335696" :question/problem "The position $x(t)$ of a particle (in meters) at time $t$ seconds is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)=t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t),\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the time at which the particle is stationary. Do not round your answer.\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+1 & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(t) & =-1 \\\\ t & ={e}^{-1} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{1}{e}\\,\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335696/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e21f194e-fb5d-5c90-af38-18d8c75582a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335696"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335894/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "cffe0db4-61fd-5cb1-aa4a-c9db7f51f30e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335894/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "37bd74e5-89bc-50e9-aea2-da380eb0c729" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335894/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-335894/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335894/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "569216e1-1ce8-59e5-8279-a03ef1a834ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335894/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "361d2fd6-e3c4-5fba-a2a1-5695993a232c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335894/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-335894/field-2/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335894" :question/id #uuid "2bbdd033-0ced-5dd1-8e93-4f0957d5c8b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335894" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6t-6}{2\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(t-1)}{2\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(t-1)}{\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}}\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ \\frac{3(t-1)}{\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}-6t+10}} & <0 \\\\ 3(t-1) & <0 \\\\ t-1 & <0 \\\\ t & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (0,1).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-335894/field-1" "q-335894/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335894"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-48909"] :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=(3t-5)^{2}$, where $t>0$ is the time measured in seconds. Determine the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((3t-5)^{2}) \\\\  & =6(3t-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve the inequality $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ 6(3t-5) & <0 \\\\ 3t-5 & <0 \\\\ 3t & <5 \\\\ t & <\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is $t\\in (0,\\frac{5}{3}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-335713/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "6cc8197e-8bdc-56a0-9784-3ca0da57f8ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335713/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d47248a-4800-5765-9ddb-343d2d3f6d68" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335713/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-335713/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335713/field-2/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer/id #uuid "a96a5135-935f-57d6-929f-d12a5564c606" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335713/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbb2e179-4bca-528c-b413-dfdfdbe6cdf2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-335713/field-2/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer-field/choices ["q-335713/field-2/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335713" :question/id #uuid "7d78f851-53b4-5dbc-b66a-4ce2e2f325b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-335713" :question/problem "The displacement $s,$ in meters, relative to a fixed origin $O$ of a particle moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis is given by $s(t)=3t{e}^{-2t},$ where $t>0$ is the time, measured in seconds. Find the time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x.$\n\n$t\\in (${{field-1}},\n\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "If the particle moves in the direction of decreasing $x,$ then its velocity is negative. \n\nFirst, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t{e}^{-2t}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-2t}+3t(-2){e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =(3-6t){e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate time intervals in which the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x$, we solve $v(t)<0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & <0 \\\\ (3-6t){e}^{-2t} & <0 \\\\ 3-6t & <0 \\\\ -6t & <-3 \\\\ t & >\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time interval in which the particle is moving to the direction of decreasing $x$ is \n\n$$\nt\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-335713/field-1" "q-335713/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f3989e-bde0-5b3a-8d45-08f34d330c3f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335713"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186261490 :question/answer-fields 17592186274965] {:db/id "q-73243/selection/ma-v1/ab25405e8a74a783afa23fa3b5d1d011e26584199d4791922af3c054872df5ff/ec47d6a09cf94925c099e26b74c2c9ae75e4527290658abb95ea8dc13bff2710" :answer/id #uuid "8a288eab-e1f2-5e59-9902-802f5d8787ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=1,\\,v=\\frac{1}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73243/selection/ma-v1/ab25405e8a74a783afa23fa3b5d1d011e26584199d4791922af3c054872df5ff/307f4e1edd127fb18e88fec90036875397878b5e7fb146338adb56cee7c6b6f3" :answer/id #uuid "0cf65e50-bb88-5c21-8317-13393f4c132b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=3,\\,v=\\frac{3}{{e}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73243/selection/ma-v1/ab25405e8a74a783afa23fa3b5d1d011e26584199d4791922af3c054872df5ff/67a52b8d58c8228579cd3b8370cc5eb3a5215b0603ef4f3be5990080fd7445a4" :answer/id #uuid "d8f9421d-8284-5c82-bcd4-0dacb6cfc98d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t=2,\\,v=\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-73243/selection/ma-v1/ab25405e8a74a783afa23fa3b5d1d011e26584199d4791922af3c054872df5ff/d9d0b03653a8c031273c89e8e1f68a3cba914aa69bea9276f05df802f2f5d15d" :answer/id #uuid "e06fa6db-c8e6-5a00-9d6c-6be9ec3bed60" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value